---
# Slice 4 fences the infra tier: traefik, authelia, crowdsec, pihole, mosquitto, nut and
# jellyfin all carry `netpol-baseline: enforced` (Task 7), each behind its own per-workload
# allow policy. This probe's question is narrower than "is anything reachable" — it asserts
# that four of those workloads (authelia, crowdsec, nut, mosquitto) refuse a caller that is not
# on their allow list, the same inverted shape as the slice-2/slice-3 probes.
#
# THIS JOB RUNS IN {{ k8s_namespace }}, same namespace as all four targets — unlike slice 3,
# there is no cross-namespace trap here to get wrong.
#
# CONTROL, not inverted: traefik:80 must succeed. The probe pod carries no label any slice-4
# policy admits, and traefik's ingress policy (networkpolicy-traefik.yaml.j2) is open to all
# sources by design (it terminates LAN/WAN traffic before Authelia or any app sees it) — so a
# reachable traefik:80 proves the probe pod has a network and DNS at all. Without this leg, a
# probe pod that itself has no network would print "isolated" for every inverted target for the
# wrong reason: not because the fence works, but because nothing works.
#
# INVERTED, same reasoning as slices 2 and 3: a failed `nc` is the PASS case. A crashlooping or
# scaled-to-zero target would report "isolated" too, so this alone cannot tell "fenced by the
# policy" apart from "nothing is listening" — the readiness gate in tasks/main.yml proves all
# four targets are up before this Job runs, closing that gap the same way the slice-2/3 gates do.
#
# Ports hardcoded from each target's own NetworkPolicy `port:` (networkpolicy-authelia.yaml.j2:26,
# networkpolicy-crowdsec.yaml.j2:26, networkpolicy-nut.yaml.j2:69,
# networkpolicy-mosquitto.yaml.j2:61) — a wrong port here fails silently green, since these
# assertions are inverted: a port nothing listens on makes `nc` fail, which is the PASS branch.
# Re-check against the Service source whenever this file is touched; the readiness gate checks
# the Service has ready backends, not that these numbers are the ports it serves.
#
# DNS leg: the name it resolves must be one ONLY pihole can answer, and picking that name is the
# whole difficulty of this leg.
#   A cluster.local name proves nothing. CoreDNS's Corefile has
#   `kubernetes cluster.local in-addr.arpa ip6.arpa { fallthrough in-addr.arpa ip6.arpa }`, so a
#   *.svc.cluster.local query is answered authoritatively and NEVER reaches the `forward .` line.
#   A pihole policy that broke external resolution would leave such a leg green.
#   A public name proves nothing either. The forward line is
#   `forward . 10.43.252.187 1.1.1.1 1.0.0.1 { policy sequential; health_check 5s; max_fails 2 }`
#   — pihole first, but Cloudflare is a live fallback, so a fenced-out pihole still resolves any
#   public name.
# `daniel-pi.lan` is the discriminator. Pi-hole serves it as a `host-record` (the shared
# ansible/templates/pihole-dnsmasq.conf.j2), and `.lan` is a private TLD Cloudflare NXDOMAINs.
# Verified live 2026-08-20: `dig @10.43.0.10 daniel-pi.lan` → 10.0.0.139, `dig @1.1.1.1
# daniel-pi.lan` → NXDOMAIN. So a successful lookup proves the query reached pihole and pihole
# answered. There is no coredns-custom ConfigMap on this cluster (checked the same day), so the
# `import /etc/coredns/custom/*.override` above the forward stubs nothing locally.
# Caveat on timing, not on correctness: the Corefile's `cache 30` can serve this answer from
# CoreDNS's cache for up to 30s after pihole becomes unreachable.
apiVersion: batch/v1
kind: Job
metadata:
  name: netpol-baseline-probe-slice4
  namespace: {{ k8s_namespace }}
spec:
  # Deliberately BELOW the `kubectl wait --timeout=180s` in tasks/main.yml, same reasoning as the
  # slice-3 probe: two independent clocks starting at different moments, so the Job's own
  # deadline must be the one that always fires first — otherwise a slow cold image pull makes it
  # a coin flip which timeout wins, and the two failures read completely differently. Costs no
  # wall time either way: with backoffLimit: 0 a failed Job never satisfies
  # --for=condition=complete, so the wait burns its full 180s regardless.
  activeDeadlineSeconds: 120
  backoffLimit: 0
  ttlSecondsAfterFinished: 86400
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: netpol-probe-slice4
        # Admits this pod to the sentinel (speedtest:80) only — see
        # networkpolicy-netpol-sentinel.yaml.j2.
        netpol-probe: control
    spec:
      restartPolicy: Never
      enableServiceLinks: false
      automountServiceAccountToken: false
      containers:
        - name: probe
          image: {{ netpol_baseline_probe_image }}
          command:
            - sh
            - -c
            - |
              set -u
              # CONTROL. This pod carries no label any slice-4 policy admits, so it must still
              # reach traefik:80 -- those ports are open to all sources by design. A failure
              # here means the probe itself has no network, and every assertion below would be
              # a false pass.
              if ! nc -w 5 -z traefik 80; then
                echo "CONTROL FAILED: traefik:80 unreachable -- assertions below are meaningless"
                exit 1
              fi
              echo "control ok: traefik:80 reachable"

              # INVERTED. Each must FAIL. An unlisted caller reaching these means the fence is
              # not doing its job.
              for target in authelia:9091 crowdsec:8080 nut:3493 mosquitto:1883; do
                host=${target%%:*}
                port=${target##*:}
                if nc -w 5 -z "$host" "$port"; then
                  echo "LEAK: $target reachable from an unlisted caller"
                  exit 1
                fi
                echo "isolated: $target unreachable from an unlisted caller"
              done

              # DNS. daniel-pi.lan is a pihole-only record: CoreDNS holds no authority for
              # `.lan`, so the query traverses `forward .`, and Cloudflare (the fallback
              # resolver on that same line) NXDOMAINs it. Resolving it therefore proves pihole
              # itself answered -- a cluster.local name would be answered by CoreDNS without
              # ever reaching pihole, and a public name would be answered by the Cloudflare
              # fallback even with pihole fenced out.
              #
              # Two forms, either of which passing is a pass. The image is
              # {{ netpol_baseline_probe_image }}, whose nslookup is BusyBox's applet, and this
              # is the first probe in the role to resolve a name outside cluster.local -- so
              # its search-list behaviour has no precedent here to lean on. `daniel-pi.lan` has
              # one dot against the pod's `ndots:5`, so a resolver that honours the search list
              # tries daniel-pi.lan.{{ k8s_namespace }}.svc.cluster.local and its siblings
              # first, and some BusyBox builds stop at that first NXDOMAIN instead of going on
              # to the absolute name. The trailing-dot form skips the search list outright. No
              # false pass either way: nothing but pihole answers `daniel-pi.lan` in any form,
              # and the search-list variants NXDOMAIN.
              if ! nslookup daniel-pi.lan. >/dev/null 2>&1 &&
                 ! nslookup daniel-pi.lan >/dev/null 2>&1; then
                echo "DNS FAILED: pihole did not answer daniel-pi.lan -- check the pihole policy"
                exit 1
              fi
              echo "dns ok: pihole answered daniel-pi.lan, so :53 is reachable through the fence"
{{ hardened_security_context() }}
{{ container_resources(cpu_limit='200m', mem_limit='64Mi', cpu_request='10m', mem_request='16Mi') }}
